This website is the reproducibility and deployment reference for the Dynamic Vision Transformer (Dynamic-ViT) pipeline originally implemented by Gabriel François, Avner El Baz and Léo Leroy, inspired by Rao et al. (2021) and built on top of the standard ViT of Dosovitskiy et al. (2021). It was then augmented with an MAE-style self-supervised stage (He et al. 2022) and a semantic alignment stage (REPA) distilling features from a frozen DINOv2 backbone (Oquab et al. 2024).
The goal of this site is not to retrain the models from scratch — training the full pipeline on ImageNet-1k requires a multi-GPU setup that is incompatible with a Continuous Integration runner. Instead, this document:

documents the project structure, its dependencies, and the configuration used;
re-renders the figures and checkpoints produced by the training scripts so that readers can inspect the results without having a GPU;
describes how to reproduce a single forward pass of the lightweight Student on any 128×128 RGB image, locally or from a fresh clone of the repo;
acts as the public entry point for CI/CD (GitHub Actions auto-publishes this site on every push to the reprod branch).







ImportantCaveats




Training the Teacher and Student models requires a CUDA-capable GPU with at least ~16 GB of VRAM and several hours of compute — it is not re-executed when this Quarto report is rendered.
All figures below are loaded from logs/imagenet/ and checkpoints/public/imagenet/, which are produced by the training scripts under src/train/.
The dataset (ImageNet-1k 128×128, benjamin-paine/imagenet-1k-128x128 on Hugging Face) is public but heavy (~30 GB); the inference demo at the end of this report only needs a handful of sample images, bundled in data/images/.

